<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN"
  "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  importers/daily_pull.py, once a working morning.

  A TEMPLATE, not a plist. `@REPO@` and `@PYTHON@` are substituted at install
  time because a committed absolute path carries a username, and this file is
  in git while the installed one is not.

  Install:

      REPO="$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)"
      mkdir -p "$REPO/reports" ~/Library/LaunchAgents
      sed -e "s|@REPO@|$REPO|g" -e "s|@PYTHON@|$(command -v python3)|g" \
        "$REPO/tools/launchd/vn.seta.insight.dailypull.plist.in" \
        > ~/Library/LaunchAgents/vn.seta.insight.dailypull.plist
      launchctl bootstrap gui/$UID \
        ~/Library/LaunchAgents/vn.seta.insight.dailypull.plist

  Remove:

      launchctl bootout gui/$UID/vn.seta.insight.dailypull
      rm ~/Library/LaunchAgents/vn.seta.insight.dailypull.plist

  Run it now rather than waiting for the clock, which is also how you find out
  whether it works:

      launchctl kickstart -p gui/$UID/vn.seta.insight.dailypull
      tail -f "$REPO/reports/daily-pull.log"


  WHY launchd AND NOT cron

  Because the machine is a laptop that is shut every evening. cron does not run
  a job it slept through; the entry fires or it does not, and a job that lands
  in the small hours never fires at all. launchd runs a missed
  StartCalendarInterval as soon as the machine wakes, so the worst case is a
  late pull rather than a missing day -- and a missing day in a scheduled cache
  is invisible, which is the failure mode this whole job exists to remove.
  Separately, cron on macOS has needed Full Disk Access granted to
  /usr/sbin/cron since Catalina, which is a per-machine GUI step nobody
  remembers having skipped.


  WHY 09:15 AND NOT OVERNIGHT

  The laptops here are awake roughly 09:00-19:00 ICT. Overnight is the obvious
  slot and it is the one hour of the day this machine is guaranteed to be
  closed. 09:15 is inside the window the machine is actually on, so the normal
  case is an on-time run and the wake-up catch-up stays a fallback.


  WHY ITS OWN LABEL

  `vn.seta.insight` is the client's hourly collection agent (cli/schedule.py).
  Sharing a label would mean `insight schedule --off` silently removing this
  job, and this job's install removing that one. That is not hypothetical: a
  bootout against the wrong label already deleted a live agent here once, on
  2026-08-26. The suffix keeps the two independent.


  NO CREDENTIALS IN THIS FILE

  ~/Library/LaunchAgents is world-readable, and launchd hands a job no shell
  environment -- so the temptation is to paste the tokens into
  EnvironmentVariables. Don't. `common.Config` walks up from its own module to
  the repository-root `.env`, which works regardless of the environment or the
  working directory. WorkingDirectory below is for the relative `reports/cache`
  path, not for finding the credentials.


  IT PUBLISHES, AND ONLY A WHOLE DAY

  `--publish` rebuilds the /insights snapshot and puts it on the endpoint after
  the pull, so the two screens follow the data with nobody typing anything. It
  needs `reports/dashboard.json` -- the member list, the prices and the
  destination -- which is gitignored because it names people; copy
  reports/dashboard.json.example. Without that file the pull still runs and the
  publish step reports the missing config and exits non-zero.

  A pull with a blocked or failed source is NOT published, and daily_pull.py
  enforces that rather than this file: a missing source is absent, not zero,
  and the screens would draw a week Bitbucket failed on as a week in which
  nobody delivered anything. The endpoint then keeps serving the previous
  snapshot, which is older and true.

  The push is scp/ssh under the login this agent runs as, with BatchMode on so
  a missing key fails instead of waiting forever for a prompt that has no
  terminal to appear on. No credential goes to the endpoint -- see below.


  NOT ON THE `future` BOX

  This is a per-user LaunchAgent on the developer laptop, which is the only
  machine that holds these credentials. `future` runs untrusted workflow code
  and must never hold one. If this were installed there anyway it would report
  four blocked sources and exit 1, rather than write a day of zeros.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key><string>vn.seta.insight.dailypull</string>
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>@PYTHON@</string>
    <string>@REPO@/importers/daily_pull.py</string>
    <!-- Update /insights and /activities from the day just pulled. Off unless
         asked for, and refused on an incomplete pull; see the header. -->
    <string>--publish</string>
  </array>
  <key>WorkingDirectory</key><string>@REPO@</string>
  <key>StartCalendarInterval</key>
  <dict>
    <key>Hour</key><integer>9</integer>
    <key>Minute</key><integer>15</integer>
  </dict>
  <!-- False, unlike the client's agent. That one uses StartInterval, which
       counts from load and so needed a run at login to avoid losing the first
       hour of every day. StartCalendarInterval is absolute and catches up on
       its own, so a run at every login would only re-read a cache the day
       already has. -->
  <key>RunAtLoad</key><false/>
  <key>StandardOutPath</key><string>@REPO@/reports/daily-pull.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key><string>@REPO@/reports/daily-pull.log</string>
  <!-- Background nice-ness and low-priority IO: this fires while somebody is
       starting work on the same machine, and a paced AIO inventory pass runs
       for minutes. -->
  <key>ProcessType</key><string>Background</string>
  <key>LowPriorityIO</key><true/>
  <key>Nice</key><integer>5</integer>
</dict>
</plist>
